:answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440010}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "08dd43ae-dafe-50fa-a506-9626463b04ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440009} :answer-field/id #uuid "c6a8eb7e-030d-57b8-89a2-4a3b521b0192" :answer-field/key "At every point $(x, y)$ where $x > 1$ and $y < - 2$, the slope of the slope field is positive" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440362}] :question/id #uuid "4026efcc-47dd-5838-ae2e-6298d2d740c1" :question/math-academy-id "q-96475" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation $\\frac{dy}{dx} = (x - 1)(y + 2)$ and its slope field. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "For (x-1)(y+2), both factors are positive in region “At every point $(x, y)$ where $x > 1$ and $y > - 2$, the slope of the slope field is positive”. In regions “At every point $(x, y)$ where $x < 1$ and $y > - 2$, the slope of the slope field is positive” and “At every point $(x, y)$ where $x > 1$ and $y < - 2$, the slope of the slope field is positive” one factor is negative and one positive. Only statement “At every point $(x, y)$ where $x > 1$ and $y > - 2$, the slope of the slope field is positive” is true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172640 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e7544c8-1881-562c-a26c-67a5f3ad2076" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/b3/b3e3fd451b0ebb2b4eb614dd54f5193be179f8bdcf7d84191004ddb80e39b3f8.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358016} {:answer/id #uuid "4b404c6e-5559-5f02-84c2-72074650e04f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/a3/a324b98fc4ec7a38ce6276a357a649751b316c0e61ddb193ce5f34caed608e98.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358397} {:answer/id #uuid "bfc84bf1-4325-5c89-9a0a-6f20097f7793" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/01/01cdcfc68fa5bf42f788b8ef9a099364ac6428457c40240bec101f18cb26ba01.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359431} {:answer/id #uuid "c6c40e04-dba8-5b50-8824-d407b26a69b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/37/37b6f122623a0f6952362446078958512ea57fb86aa04aa735b93487a7fdecb1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359500} {:answer/id #uuid "db446cd3-68d8-5b4e-8744-b1c105346c32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/d3/d3fc1db67353f797a60edd29987d01f6798bac25d98664845d9541635828410d.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359670}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4b404c6e-5559-5f02-84c2-72074650e04f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/a3/a324b98fc4ec7a38ce6276a357a649751b316c0e61ddb193ce5f34caed608e98.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358397} :answer-field/id #uuid "79f71ba8-1e73-5288-aa74-8336d55348a0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360194}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "8e4e2c73-61a2-51eb-9aeb-b173fc18ef10" :question/math-academy-id "q-96476" :question/problem "Which of the following could be the slope field for the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=xy?$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that we are given an equation of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(x,y).\n$$\n\nThe right-hand side is a function of $x$ and $y.$ This means that the slope varies with both $x$ and $y.$\n\nOf the given slope fields, the only one that varies with both $x$ and $y$ is the following:\n\n![](images/a3/a324b98fc4ec7a38ce6276a357a649751b316c0e61ddb193ce5f34caed608e98.png)\n\n \n\nTo see this more clearly, notice that if we draw a vertical line and a horizontal line, the slopes vary if we move along either of the two lines.\n\n![](images/9d/9d60c65cda12bb6ac800906e7889a8ecc395974dff0379130d647810c1623869.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172641 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "866243bf-f149-5fa2-95ed-23db86ef9c18" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/98/989a9c0a5f720929dda562c4b8f64aa3cc84001d53ca2b3be8ad6cd53f458c5d.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171472} {:answer/id #uuid "abcc77e5-1280-52fb-886a-7633a10799eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/43/43d4b05227a0667420e6978f01671931fb2392fe39692400407708edc8c24987.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171473} {:answer/id #uuid "aeda3f8e-03ae-56a1-85d4-19b7a4ea1942" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/78/78042a69ce4ac14b6d99455b32d40e35830efb1086a3c3fc05f705988b913b40.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171474} {:answer/id #uuid "1199c78f-82c4-59d3-bd76-979d091ea864" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/3e/3e8b9711bda744d16af71f3c4ae6ddb4e2007f6dc2963c87f7d643831209b8c7.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171475} {:answer/id #uuid "222bf516-0942-5beb-9b32-72ba7936029b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/54/543bb98ff948d1186e05d84db7992f9d5033ba4eaf47a9787b6b5664b88510a2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171476}] :answer-field/id #uuid "76a21e6d-b63f-5600-9fb8-76bffb74c9f8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171471}] :question/id #uuid "b270605a-12cb-50fa-a020-fc0b43b612d7" :question/math-academy-id "q-96477" :question/problem "Given that $f(x, y)$ varies with both $x$ and $y$, which of the following **cannot** be the slope field for the differential equation $\\frac{dy}{dx} = f(x, y)$?"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172645 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "817b7d0e-081e-5f65-8394-7b2aa4d4b020" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438622} {:answer/id #uuid "51e53bdb-ad9b-5fde-96ad-6776ae72ee75" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438623}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "51e53bdb-ad9b-5fde-96ad-6776ae72ee75" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438623} :answer-field/id #uuid "87784eaa-7f37-5cfd-bf94-f676298d7eb1" :answer-field/key "At every point in the first quadrant, the slope of the slope field is positive" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439340} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "434b294c-ea0b-5331-849a-fa7706de7c3c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438624} {:answer/id #uuid "a7afa29d-56b9-57cf-9491-c30536a33167" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438625}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "434b294c-ea0b-5331-849a-fa7706de7c3c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438624} :answer-field/id #uuid "c8a8760c-7b07-5de6-8cb4-15b2d479532a" :answer-field/key "At every point in the second quadrant, the slope of the slope field is positive" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439341} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "005ad32f-fad3-5e4b-9876-1dea386fda92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438626} {:answer/id #uuid "da8dc176-0ecf-5a7b-9dd5-659d6a397554" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438627}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "da8dc176-0ecf-5a7b-9dd5-659d6a397554" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438627} :answer-field/id #uuid "83dc6e63-ac75-598d-8eb8-29e5e8e60f37" :answer-field/key "At every point in the third quadrant, the slope of the slope field is positive" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439342}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "fd7005de-da4a-5aae-a0ea-f0c01c178438" :question/math-academy-id "q-96767" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=2xy$ and its slope field. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "First, we define $f(x,y)=2xy.$ Then, our differential equation is $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(x,y).$ \n\nNotice that $f(x,y)=0$ when $x=0$ or $y=0.$ The lines $x=0$ and $y=0$ divide the plane into the usual four quadrants.\n\nIf we compute the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each quadrant (by considering some test points, for example), we get the following:\n\n![](images/6e/6ef852c9c15dc6f591119906abe479949f929edb6dc26f21588b4de545d02da2.png)\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement:\n\n- Statement “At every point in the first quadrant, the slope of the slope field is positive” is true. If $(x,y)$ is in the first quadrant, then according to our diagram, we have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(x,y)}=f(x,y)>0.\\,\n$$\n\nThis implies that the slope of the slope field is positive for any point $(x,y)$ in the first quadrant.\n\n- Statement “At every point in the second quadrant, the slope of the slope field is positive” is false. If $(x,y)$ is in the second quadrant, then according to our diagram, we have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(x,y)}=f(x,y)<0.\\,\n$$\n\nThis implies that the slope of the slope field is negative for any point $(x,y)$ in the second quadrant.\n\n- Statement “At every point in the third quadrant, the slope of the slope field is positive” is true. If $(x,y)$ is in the third quadrant, then according to our diagram, we have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(x,y)}=f(x,y)>0.\\,\n$$\n\nThis implies that the slope of the slope field is positive for any point $(x,y)$ in the third quadrant."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172648 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a3c0e91a-8719-5dbf-92fb-86a7721ef654" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440023} {:answer/id #uuid "55bf2da5-d708-5480-8ab8-1441064688e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440024}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a3c0e91a-8719-5dbf-92fb-86a7721ef654" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440023} :answer-field/id #uuid "402941ab-b608-5343-ad5f-8ac1f83f99c2" :answer-field/key "At every point in the first quadrant, the slope of the slope field is negative" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440369} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e82d40ad-c41b-57a8-a872-ff25ef67196e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440025} {:answer/id #uuid "fa689d24-4924-55c6-8219-2928fe0f7962" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440026}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fa689d24-4924-55c6-8219-2928fe0f7962" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440026} :answer-field/id #uuid "097f13ad-dd16-5c08-84cd-9bb354ae065e" :answer-field/key "At every point in the second quadrant, the slope of the slope field is negative" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440370} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "934c3087-576f-525a-b55f-b5b5ad9f17fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440027} {:answer/id #uuid "926d422e-445c-57ec-9858-ba3785eafd46" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440028}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "926d422e-445c-57ec-9858-ba3785eafd46" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440028} :answer-field/id #uuid "f873dbf2-94f8-5412-b40f-fa9306104dc1" :answer-field/key "At every point in the third quadrant, the slope of the slope field is negative" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440371}] :question/id #uuid "1142a38e-b72d-5615-beec-20a68972aab2" :question/math-academy-id "q-97483" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation $\\frac{dy}{dx} = xy^{2}$ and its slope field. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "For xy^2, y^2 is positive in every open quadrant, so the slope has the sign of x. It is negative in quadrants II and III, and positive in quadrant I."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172649 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d00f36e2-7dd8-58b5-a5b1-0ebba7aa6933" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438658} {:answer/id #uuid "a46a6654-913e-5d83-b11d-f2b6cc46331e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438659}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a46a6654-913e-5d83-b11d-f2b6cc46331e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438659} :answer-field/id #uuid "b9fcc13e-f640-566c-9e43-967f6aad6e96" :answer-field/key "At every point $(x,y)$ where $x<-1$ and $y>2,$ the slope of the slope field is positive" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439358} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8be5feeb-9d33-592c-a7c3-de2eabcecb75" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438660} {:answer/id #uuid "ba8012c8-d490-5f67-b39d-c4d8b2098520" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438661}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ba8012c8-d490-5f67-b39d-c4d8b2098520" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438661} :answer-field/id #uuid "f9ecebbf-d075-56b9-875f-97282f7006cc" :answer-field/key "At every point $(x,y)$ where $x<-1$ and $y<2,$ the slope of the slope field is negative" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439359} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7480480b-28cb-58dd-9d62-47133cd8e084" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438662} {:answer/id #uuid "26b8d424-6ae7-5f06-89f8-162aeb05ca51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438663}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "26b8d424-6ae7-5f06-89f8-162aeb05ca51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438663} :answer-field/id #uuid "7c7ae5f0-6a16-510b-b3e1-9d80c0ffee45" :answer-field/key "At every point $(x,y)$ where $x>-1$ and $y<2,$ the slope of the slope field is negative" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439360}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "e86fafab-049b-51e4-93da-aada8761c377" :question/math-academy-id "q-97538" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=(x+1)^{2}(y-2)$ and its slope field. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "First, we define $f(x,y)=(x+1)^{2}(y-2).$ Then, our differential equation is $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(x,y).$ \n\nNotice that $f(x,y)=0$ when $x=-1$ or $y=2.$ This divides the $xy$-plane into four regions, as shown below.\n\n![](images/1f/1f4b1b2ca736fb83eff0a4b85a3f479bf99b3b337492d0a77abe739038ed141f.png)\n\nIf we compute the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each of those four regions (by considering some test points, for example), we get the following:\n\n![](images/fd/fdafadd1631a75c2998aaed5c6bad62a3ea1dcc0ac57d245a00d167e74aa3bb4.png)\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement:\n\n- Statement “At every point $(x,y)$ where $x<-1$ and $y>2,$ the slope of the slope field is positive” is true. If $x<-1$ and $y>2,$ then according to our diagram, we have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(x,y)}=f(x,y)>0.\n$$\n\nThis implies that the slope of the slope field is positive for any point $(x,y)$ in this region.\n\n- Statement “At every point $(x,y)$ where $x<-1$ and $y<2,$ the slope of the slope field is negative” is true. If $x<-1$ and $y<2,$ then according to our diagram, we have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(x,y)}=f(x,y)<0.\n$$\n\nThis implies that the slope of the slope field is negative for any point $(x,y)$ in this region.\n\n- Statement “At every point $(x,y)$ where $x>-1$ and $y<2,$ the slope of the slope field is negative” is true. If $x>-1$ and $y<2,$ then according to our diagram, we have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(x,y)}=f(x,y)<0.\n$$\n\nThis implies that the slope of the slope field is negative for any point $(x,y)$ in this region."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175693 :question/id #uuid "70569afd-42c4-5e5f-8397-4fbd873eb8e4" :question/math-academy-id "e-11002" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $y=\\cos x?$ Assume that $n$ is an integer.\n\n- The range of the function is $[-1,1].$\n\n- The function has minimum values at $x =n\\pi.$\n\n- The function intersects the $x$-axis at $x=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2} +\\pi n.$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's plot the graph of $y=\\cos{x}.$\n\n![](images/88/88378ece5b1721cdc533d07d69c004901539c7f8fda9a5a3e5c5648ed32905c5.png)\n\nNow, let's go through each statement.\n\n- Statement I is true. The minimum value is $-1,$ and the maximum value is $1.$ Therefore, the range is $[-1,1].$\n\n- Statement II is false. The function has a minimum at $x=\\pi$ and the period is $2\\pi.$ Therefore, the minimum values occur at $\\pi+2n\\pi.$\n\n- Statement III is true. These are the zeros of the cosine curve. The cosine curve has zeros at $x=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2} + n\\pi.$\n\nIn conclusion, only statements I and III are true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175694 :question/id #uuid "a8cc8b8f-463b-5734-a33d-e915159cba32" :question/math-academy-id "e-11003" :question/problem "If $\\cos x =- \\dfrac {1}{3},$ find the value of $2\\cos( x + 4\\pi)+3.$" :question/worked-solution "The period of $y = \\cos x$ is $2\\pi.$ Therefore, for any integer $n$ we have\n\n$$\n\\cos{x} = \\cos{(x+2n\\pi)} .\n$$\n\nSubstituting $n=2$ into the right-hand-side of the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}cos⁡x & =cos⁡(x+2\\cdot 2\\cdot \\pi ) \\\\  & =cos⁡(x+4\\pi ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, if $\\cos{x} =- \\dfrac {1}{3},$ then $\\cos{(x+4\\pi)} = - \\dfrac {1}{3}.$\n\nFinally, then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2cos⁡(x+4\\pi )+3 & =2\\left(-\\frac{1}{3}\\right)+3 \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}+3 \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175703 :question/id #uuid "08253dde-510a-5451-ba5c-64d42d647db2" :question/math-academy-id "e-11012" :question/problem "If $\\cos x = \\dfrac{\\sqrt 3}{4},$ then find the value of $\\cos (-x-6\\pi).$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the graph of $y=\\cos{x}.$\n\n![](images/23/2373daf4b5fd26f098bf90e998e2685db6a8b793e94ac9d80b08ea423cb535c0.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we note the following:\n\n- The period of $\\cos{x}$ is $2\\pi.$\n\n- Our graph is symmetric about the $y$-axis. This means that $\\cos{x}$ is an *even* function, and we have\n\n$$\n\\cos(-x) = \\cos{x}.\n$$\n\nLet's now evaluate our expression:\n\n- Firstly, using the periodicity of $\\cos{x},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}cos⁡(-x-6\\pi ) & =cos⁡(-x-2\\pi \\cdot 3) \\\\  & =cos⁡(-x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Secondly, using the evenness of $\\cos{x},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}cos⁡(-x) & =cos⁡x \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\cos (-x-6\\pi)= \\dfrac{\\sqrt 3}{4}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176346 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "95bf4225-7f2b-533b-b53e-c9b1fee93f68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267480} {:answer/id #uuid "9832c278-c6fe-5a16-af70-2a5d2cbee4ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267511} {:answer/id #uuid "ad699b9a-4479-5d4d-a163-8f41699cbfeb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267711} {:answer/id #uuid "e1ef3bf2-09ff-56a5-a1d9-4ee8d61fd861" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268141} {:answer/id #uuid "e58c1d7f-af4f-5dd4-b8bb-d3a27371d68e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268174}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e58c1d7f-af4f-5dd4-b8bb-d3a27371d68e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268174} :answer-field/id #uuid "caecf20f-30a6-5b32-b66b-f7b3637a7847" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268771}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "195eeaaa-ad0f-5b04-bea4-14b7076ff8c6" :question/math-academy-id "q-136545" :question/problem "If $cos⁡a=-\\frac{1}{3},$ then $cos⁡(-a)=$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the graph of $y=cos⁡x.$\n\n![](images/23/2373daf4b5fd26f098bf90e998e2685db6a8b793e94ac9d80b08ea423cb535c0.png)\n\nNotice that our graph is symmetric about the $y$-axis. If we reflect the graph over the $y$-axis, we get the same picture. This means that $cos⁡x$ is an *even* function, and we have\n\n$$\ncos⁡(-x)=cos⁡x.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}cos⁡(-a) & =cos⁡a \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176355 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8740d42c-af72-54d3-a6c1-301e4a90425d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{24} + 8π" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174389} {:answer/id #uuid "a1033a8a-9371-5723-b267-c44596b6